["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Before AI spirals out of control, Plan A is the only brake that can still be applied in time Plan A uses research transparency, compute limits, and citizen dividends to pull AI back from exponential explosion to a controllable slope; the author himself estimates it still carries a 15% chance of catastrophe, but it beats sitting back and watching AI take off. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours AI 2027&#x27;s author returns with a plan to change the ending | Daniel Kokotajlo AI governance superintelligence AGI timeline Plan A alignment compute control Plan A horizon-length neuralese superpersuasion privacy-preserving auditing mutual assured compute destruction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-eightythousand-plan-a-和-ai-赛跑的最不坏方案/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/83e62d301b70a349-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">3:47:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Before AI spirals out of control, Plan A is the only brake that can still be applied in time</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plan A uses research transparency, compute limits, and citizen dividends to pull AI back from exponential explosion to a controllable slope; the author himself estimates it still carries a 15% chance of catastrophe, but it beats sitting back and watching AI take off.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI governance</span><span class=\"tag\">superintelligence</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Takeover Isn&#x27;t a 2029 Problem—It&#x27;s a 2028 Problem Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated between 2028 and 2031, with progress quadrupling or quintupling annually, making takeover risk extreme; he proposes a US-China compute transparency agreement as a slowdown plan. The MAD Podcast MAD Podcast AI Could Take Over in 2029. Is It Already Too Late? | Ryan Greenblatt AI risk superintelligence prediction compute governance valuation impact GDP growth Zuckerberg RSI / recursive self-improvement Reward hacking Hill climbing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-madpod-ai-全面接管或发生在-2029-治理窗口只剩三年/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/90800bca4b008abf-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:18:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The MAD Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Takeover Isn&#x27;t a 2029 Problem—It&#x27;s a 2028 Problem</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated between 2028 and 2031, with progress quadrupling or quintupling annually, making takeover risk extreme; he proposes a US-China compute transparency agreement as a slowdown plan.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI risk</span><span class=\"tag\">superintelligence prediction</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"After GLP-1: Injections Are No Longer a Barrier, Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era Injectable drugs are no longer a barrier; peptide drugs are expanding from weight loss to exercise mimetics, neural regulation, and beyond. Scientists caution: beyond GLP, data are still sparse, and approval cycles could take up to 20 years. From Our Neurons to Yours From Our Neurons to Yours After Ozempic: the revolution in peptide science | Jonathan Long peptide drugs GLP-1 exercise mimetics weight loss drug discovery biotech GLP-1 Lac-Phe Unimolecular polyagonist AGRP neurons POMC neurons MOTRPAC\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-fromourneurons-glp-1之后-肽科学正在开启医学新大门/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/372ab8e78a5e10ef-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"F\"><span class=\"dur\">49:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">From Our Neurons to Yours</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>After GLP-1: Injections Are No Longer a Barrier, Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Injectable drugs are no longer a barrier; peptide drugs are expanding from weight loss to exercise mimetics, neural regulation, and beyond. Scientists caution: beyond GLP, data are still sparse, and approval cycles could take up to 20 years.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">peptide drugs</span><span class=\"tag\">GLP-1</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Long-end rates staying high isn&#x27;t policy weakness—it&#x27;s AI data centers competing for funds Kansas City Fed President Schmid said at Jackson Hole that the 30-year Treasury yield above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for funds; his personal view is that monetary conditions remain loose, and inflation running at mid-3% must be addressed. Odd Lots Odd Lots Kansas City Fed President Jeffrey Schmid on the First Jackson Hole of the Warsh Era monetary policy inflation long-end rates Federal Reserve AI infrastructure payments innovation atomic settlement r-star reaction function forward guidance dual mandate dissent\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-oddlots-长端利率高企不是政策软弱-是实体需求在抢资金/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">28:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Long-end rates staying high isn&#x27;t policy weakness—it&#x27;s AI data centers competing for funds</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Kansas City Fed President Schmid said at Jackson Hole that the 30-year Treasury yield above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for funds; his personal view is that monetary conditions remain loose, and inflation running at mid-3% must be addressed.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">monetary policy</span><span class=\"tag\">inflation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"No Models, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning Giants with Interface and Speed Cursor&#x27;s success is not about proprietary models but about making the product itself the moat: no plugins, no early enterprise push, no self-trained models—catching up from behind through iteration speed, hiring philosophy, and founder density. The a16z Show a16z Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup Cursor AI coding startup strategy enterprise sales talent acquisition product philosophy self-serve back channel talent via acquisition monorepo\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-a16z-不做模型不早卖企业-用界面和速度赢下巨头/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">39:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Models, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning Giants with Interface and Speed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cursor&#x27;s success is not about proprietary models but about making the product itself the moat: no plugins, no early enterprise push, no self-trained models—catching up from behind through iteration speed, hiring philosophy, and founder density.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span><span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Google Paid $10M for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era In the AI era, a company&#x27;s most valuable asset is its data—Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s data; AI agents with legitimate access are the new biggest threat, and data infrastructure must be rebuilt. No Priors No Priors Rethinking Legacy Data Infrastructure with Eon Co-Founders Ofir Ehrlich and Gonen Stein Data Assets AI Security Enterprise Data AI Agents Cloud Migration NHI / Non-Human Identity PLG / Product-Led Growth Data Foundation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-nopriors-数据是-ai-时代唯一护城河-合法权限代理最危险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c958bc100db9c41b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">34:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">No Priors</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Google Paid $10M for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In the AI era, a company&#x27;s most valuable asset is its data—Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s data; AI agents with legitimate access are the new biggest threat, and data infrastructure must be rebuilt.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Data Assets</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Index Funds Pay 4% More on Rebalancing: Passive Investing Is More Than Just Indexing David Booth breaks down the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes stock prices up 4%, true passivity is scientific pricing plus fine execution; the 10% annual stock return over a century is driven by humanity&#x27;s desire to improve life. The Rational Reminder Podcast Rational Reminder 50 Years of Evidence-Based Investing (with David Booth) | Rational Reminder 424 Passive Investing Index Funds Trading Costs Investment Philosophy Dimensional Jensen&#x27;s alpha soft dollar beta passive vs indexed\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-rationalreminder-指数基金为收盘价多付4-被动投资不只是指数/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8948b5b54aada07c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Rational Reminder Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Index Funds Pay 4% More on Rebalancing: Passive Investing Is More Than Just Indexing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Booth breaks down the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes stock prices up 4%, true passivity is scientific pricing plus fine execution; the 10% annual stock return over a century is driven by humanity&#x27;s desire to improve life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Passive Investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Index Funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"What the Fed fears most is not tariffs but unanchored inflation expectations Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest risk: after five and a half years above target, the AI narrative may push rates up in the short term rather than down; his key advice for rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market. Masters of Scale Masters of Scale Chicago Fed President on inflation, recession, and Trump’s attacks Inflation expectations Federal Reserve Interest rates AI bubble Productivity Macro decisions Forward guidance Core inflation Unanchoring of inflation expectations Stagflation Lump of labor fallacy FOMC\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-masterscale-通胀预期失锚-美联储最怕的不是关税/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b728f390557a15d4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">35:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Masters of Scale</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>What the Fed fears most is not tariffs but unanchored inflation expectations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest risk: after five and a half years above target, the AI narrative may push rates up in the short term rather than down; his key advice for rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inflation expectations</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Sunscreen Toxicity Myth Debunked: Entering Blood Isn&#x27;t Harmful, but Vitamin D Risk Is Real The most-cited evidence for &#x27;sunscreen is toxic and causes cancer&#x27; mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causality; chemical sunscreen entering the blood doesn&#x27;t mean it&#x27;s toxic. But daily application does increase the risk of vitamin D deficiency in winter, and evidence for preventing melanoma in darker skin is especially weak. Science Vs Science Vs Sunscreen: Could It Be Toxic?? sunscreen vitamin D melanoma chemical sunscreen evidence-based medicine skin cancer oxybenzone UV index Fitzpatrick scale SPF mineral sunscreen RCT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-sciencevs-防晒霜不是毒药-但深肤色人群可能真不用防黑素瘤/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a3282246c3202bd6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Sunscreen Toxicity Myth Debunked: Entering Blood Isn&#x27;t Harmful, but Vitamin D Risk Is Real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The most-cited evidence for &#x27;sunscreen is toxic and causes cancer&#x27; mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causality; chemical sunscreen entering the blood doesn&#x27;t mean it&#x27;s toxic. But daily application does increase the risk of vitamin D deficiency in winter, and evidence for preventing melanoma in darker skin is especially weak.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">sunscreen</span><span class=\"tag\">vitamin D</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"No Plate Tectonics, No Life: Earth Is a Lucky Planet Plate tectonics reshapes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed of fingernail growth, generating and destroying about 3 square kilometers of oceanic crust each year; it alters climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists—without it, there would be no life. In Our Time In Our Time Archive: Plate Tectonics Plate Tectonics Earth Science Origin of Life Scientific Revolution Gaia Theory Planetary Habitability Benioff zone seafloor spreading Vine–Matthews hypothesis mantle plume black smoker Gaia theory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-inourtime-没有板块构造就没有生命-地球是幸运的行星/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c3f143ddc011b622-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">44:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">In Our Time</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Plate Tectonics, No Life: Earth Is a Lucky Planet</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plate tectonics reshapes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed of fingernail growth, generating and destroying about 3 square kilometers of oceanic crust each year; it alters climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists—without it, there would be no life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Plate Tectonics</span><span class=\"tag\">Earth Science</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Ketogenic Diet for Mental Illness: The Key Is Not Weight Loss but Mitochondria Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to help patients with treatment-resistant mental illness; the key is not just weight loss but changing brain energy metabolism and neurotransmitters through mitophagy and biogenesis. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Essentials: Diet &amp; Nutrition for Mental Health | Dr. Chris Palmer ketogenic diet mental health mitochondria low-carb clinical cases Mitophagy Mitochondrial biogenesis Ketosis Schizoaffective disorder\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-huberman-生酮饮食治疗难治性精神疾病-线粒体是核心/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/10ab5bacdd6a5f8c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">38:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Ketogenic Diet for Mental Illness: The Key Is Not Weight Loss but Mitochondria</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to help patients with treatment-resistant mental illness; the key is not just weight loss but changing brain energy metabolism and neurotransmitters through mitophagy and biogenesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">ketogenic diet</span><span class=\"tag\">mental health</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The 15,000 in the Cobalt Mine: Your Phone Battery Is Stained with Blood The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and EVs depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to Lumumba&#x27;s assassination to today&#x27;s few dollars a day, the same history of plunder continues. Throughline Throughline The pillage that fueled America&#x27;s tech booms cobalt Congo supply chain colonial history EVs Congo Free State Katanga Congo Reform Association\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-throughline-没有刚果的钴-就没有你的智能手机/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/19b38564c3e319e4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The 15,000 in the Cobalt Mine: Your Phone Battery Is Stained with Blood</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and EVs depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to Lumumba&#x27;s assassination to today&#x27;s few dollars a day, the same history of plunder continues.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">cobalt</span><span class=\"tag\">Congo</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &#x27;AI smell.&#x27; What&#x27;s truly under threat are social processes that assume &#x27;writing a letter&#x27; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction disappears, institutions overload. Complex Systems Complex Systems How AI detection actually works, with Pangram’s Max Spero AI detection text attribution anti-spam public services coding careers model ethics anti-memetic property mode collapse cognitive surplus dead internet theory Jevons Paradox humanizer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-complexsys-ai-检测的本质是作者识别-模板信博弈已失效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d5836ffb777d7c36-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &#x27;AI smell.&#x27; What&#x27;s truly under threat are social processes that assume &#x27;writing a letter&#x27; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction disappears, institutions overload.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI detection</span><span class=\"tag\">text attribution</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Open-Source Models Pass Through Five or Six Intermediaries—Who Fills the Accountability Gap in the EU AI Act? Open weights don&#x27;t equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemptions may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital. The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast Building Trustworthy Open Source AI - Sebnem Erener (EP 38) Open Source EU AI Act AI Governance Open Source Ethics European Tech Sovereignty Chain of Responsibility zero trust open washing general purpose AI models layered responsibility\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theinbetweente-开放权重不等于可信-五六个中介后谁来负责/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/99f7b27239fc36aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Open-Source Models Pass Through Five or Six Intermediaries—Who Fills the Accountability Gap in the EU AI Act?</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Open weights don&#x27;t equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemptions may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Open Source</span><span class=\"tag\">EU AI Act</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed was postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system. The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science Jahnavi Phalkey on India&#x27;s Place in the History of Modern Science history of science India big science nuclear physics cyclotron state and science Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) Coulomb excitation Big Science global majority CERN\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-thehpspodcastc-别用1974年核试验倒读印度核物理史/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d0bc1b9f1ca1b0b2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed was postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">history of science</span><span class=\"tag\">India</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Agents Accidentally Breach Their Own Systems: Fully Autonomous Attacks Are Now a Reality During an internal security evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents collaborated via a shared message board, accidentally breached their own Artifactory, and the incident spilled over to HuggingFace; the event proves that fully autonomous attacks are now a reality, and defenses must be equally automated. The AI Policy Podcast The AI Policy Podcast Responding to AI Agent Containment Failures with CSET&#x27;s Helen Toner, LawAI&#x27;s Mackenzie Arnold &amp; CSIS&#x27;s Matt Pearl AI Security Agent Collaboration Zero-Day Exploit Incident Response Cloud Infrastructure HuggingFace Artifactory SSRF RCE TOCTOU WebDAV IMDS\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theaipolicypod-ai-agent-协作失控-安全评估意外攻破内外网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bc33d180be3b8ef6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The AI Policy Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Agents Accidentally Breach Their Own Systems: Fully Autonomous Attacks Are Now a Reality</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">During an internal security evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents collaborated via a shared message board, accidentally breached their own Artifactory, and the incident spilled over to HuggingFace; the event proves that fully autonomous attacks are now a reality, and defenses must be equally automated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent Collaboration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Vandals Were Not Barbarians: Cutting Off Tax Revenue Was the Real Death of the Western Roman Empire Early Vandals were a small tribe that lost nine out of ten battles in Roman records, yet by seizing North Africa and cutting off Rome&#x27;s tax revenue, they sacked Rome in 455; the word &quot;vandalism&quot; was a political metaphor invented during the French Revolution. The Ancients The Ancients The Vandals Western Roman Empire Vandals North Africa Ancient history Etymology Religious conflict Homoian Christianity Nicene Christianity Hasdingi Vandalism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-ancients-十战九败的汪达尔人-靠断供税收终结西罗马/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2e9a1e98d4f9d33b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">50:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Vandals Were Not Barbarians: Cutting Off Tax Revenue Was the Real Death of the Western Roman Empire</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Early Vandals were a small tribe that lost nine out of ten battles in Roman records, yet by seizing North Africa and cutting off Rome&#x27;s tax revenue, they sacked Rome in 455; the word &quot;vandalism&quot; was a political metaphor invented during the French Revolution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Western Roman Empire</span><span class=\"tag\">Vandals</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Air War of WWI Was Not Romance, It Was a Meat Grinder Where Pilots Lasted Two Weeks on Average At the start of WWI, aircraft were dismissed as a joke by the officer class; within four years, the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing, and dogfighting, and in 1917 the average life expectancy of a new pilot was about two weeks. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Brutal History of The First Warplanes WWI air combat technological iteration fighter aces military history psychological trauma Pusher Tractor Interrupter gear Scout Flying circus\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-historyhit-一战空战浪漫背后-新飞行员平均活两周/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4fac589db0b89acd-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">50:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Air War of WWI Was Not Romance, It Was a Meat Grinder Where Pilots Lasted Two Weeks on Average</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">At the start of WWI, aircraft were dismissed as a joke by the officer class; within four years, the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing, and dogfighting, and in 1917 the average life expectancy of a new pilot was about two weeks.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWI air combat</span><span class=\"tag\">technological iteration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Britain Was Almost Always Ready to Accept Something Like the Belfast Agreement By 1972, Heath&#x27;s cabinet had options on the table—ending partition, a constitutional link with Ireland. It wasn&#x27;t British intransigence; it was IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 700. The Troubles: Bloody Sunday (Part 4) Northern Ireland conflict history politics military failure IRA British politics ethnic conflict Direct Rule Special Category Status Provisional IRA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-restishistory-英国当年几乎随时接受类似-贝尔法斯特协议-的安排/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:24:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Britain Was Almost Always Ready to Accept Something Like the Belfast Agreement</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">By 1972, Heath&#x27;s cabinet had options on the table—ending partition, a constitutional link with Ireland. It wasn&#x27;t British intransigence; it was IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Northern Ireland conflict</span><span class=\"tag\">history politics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Lattice Proofs Approach the Square-Root Barrier: Worst-Case Lattice Crypto Still Safe An OpenAI model proved CVP remains NP-hard under an n^(1/400) approximation factor, pushed within days to the square root; SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n, McEliece hit by a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto stays safe due to dimension blow-up and the approximation-factor gap. Security Cryptography Whatever Security Cryptography Whatever AI Lattice Proofs with Chris Peikert lattice crypto post-quantum crypto AI math cryptanalysis quantum algorithms McEliece CVP / Closest Vector Problem SVP / Shortest Vector Problem Dihedral coset problem Reed-Solomon code Information set decoding Doubly efficient PIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-securitycrypto-ai-证明封死-cvp-近似困难-gap-但不烧密码学/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a0ce9b13c93eb0a9-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Security Cryptography Whatever</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Lattice Proofs Approach the Square-Root Barrier: Worst-Case Lattice Crypto Still Safe</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An OpenAI model proved CVP remains NP-hard under an n^(1/400) approximation factor, pushed within days to the square root; SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n, McEliece hit by a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto stays safe due to dimension blow-up and the approximation-factor gap.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">lattice crypto</span><span class=\"tag\">post-quantum crypto</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Programmer Value Shifts to Directing Agents: DHH Wrote an Entire Product with AI DHH has written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI and found a counterintuitive conclusion: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel; what&#x27;s truly scarce is judgment, aesthetics, and architectural constraints. Lex Fridman Podcast Lex Fridman #501 – DHH: Future of Programming, AI, Agentic Engineering, Vibe Coding &amp; Linux AI programming Agent Linux Omachi Programming philosophy Personal software vibe coding agentic engineering harness fast takeoff bullshit jobs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-lex-程序员的价值从写码转向调度-agent/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d67cbc9ec26fc241-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">5:21:57</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Lex Fridman Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Programmer Value Shifts to Directing Agents: DHH Wrote an Entire Product with AI</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DHH has written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI and found a counterintuitive conclusion: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel; what&#x27;s truly scarce is judgment, aesthetics, and architectural constraints.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI programming</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Software 10x Slower: Root Cause Is Serial Dependencies in Architecture, Not Code Software that is 10 to 100 times slower than necessary is rarely a code-trick problem; it&#x27;s a serial dependency chain set at the architecture stage. The right optimization is to lock the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and measure the gap—provided the architecture decision-makers understand assembly. The Pragmatic Engineer The Pragmatic Engineer Why performant code matters (but gets widely ignored), with Casey Muratori Performance optimization Architecture decisions Theoretical peak AI coding Compiler optimization theoretical peak serial dependency chain hotspot branch prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-pragmatic-性能优化不靠剖析热点-靠理论峰值反推差距/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b99e79b803441ca6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:52:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Pragmatic Engineer</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Software 10x Slower: Root Cause Is Serial Dependencies in Architecture, Not Code</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Software that is 10 to 100 times slower than necessary is rarely a code-trick problem; it&#x27;s a serial dependency chain set at the architecture stage. The right optimization is to lock the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and measure the gap—provided the architecture decision-makers understand assembly.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Performance optimization</span><span class=\"tag\">Architecture decisions</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"edu\" data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s Real Shortage Isn&#x27;t Compute—It&#x27;s Self-Doubt Large models aren&#x27;t without probability; they predict probabilities at every step, but they don&#x27;t treat uncertainty as a tool for seeing their own limits. To make real decisions, they first need to build self-doubt into their architecture. Google DeepMind Google DeepMind The mathematics of AI uncertainty uncertainty Bayesian self-doubt large models weather forecasting trustworthy AI Bayesian updating aleatoric uncertainty semantic entropy calibration catastrophic forgetting\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-googledeepmind-ai-真正缺的不是算力-是自我怀疑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b3109e2d16c60ca5-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">45:04</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"edu\"><span class=\"src\">Google DeepMind</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s Real Shortage Isn&#x27;t Compute—It&#x27;s Self-Doubt</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Large models aren&#x27;t without probability; they predict probabilities at every step, but they don&#x27;t treat uncertainty as a tool for seeing their own limits. To make real decisions, they first need to build self-doubt into their architecture.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">uncertainty</span><span class=\"tag\">Bayesian</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s Next Step Isn&#x27;t Bigger Models, but Learning to Admit Uncertainty DeepMind researcher Zubin&#x27;s 30 years of research shows that current large models&#x27; &quot;confident nonsense&quot; stems from a lack of explicit probabilistic representation; Bayesian updating, predictive ensembles, and calibration are key to reliable AI and AGI. Google DeepMind: The Podcast Google DeepMind The mathematics of AI uncertainty Uncertainty Bayesian Calibration LLM Continual Learning AGI aleatoric uncertainty semantic entropy posterior beliefs calibration\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-deepmind-ai-的下一步不是更大模型-而是学会承认不确定/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e4ddf810493203-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">44:40</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Google DeepMind: The Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s Next Step Isn&#x27;t Bigger Models, but Learning to Admit Uncertainty</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DeepMind researcher Zubin&#x27;s 30 years of research shows that current large models&#x27; &quot;confident nonsense&quot; stems from a lack of explicit probabilistic representation; Bayesian updating, predictive ensembles, and calibration are key to reliable AI and AGI.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Uncertainty</span><span class=\"tag\">Bayesian</span>\n</div></div></a>"]